        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $V=V_2+V_{\\mathrm b}$ with real $V_2\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ and real $V_{\\mathrm b}\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, and put $H_0=-\\Delta$ on $H^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$. Assume that\n\n$$h(r)=\\|M_V(H_0-i)^{-1}\\mathbf1_{\\{|x|\\ge r\\}}\\|$$\n\nis finite for every $r\\ge0$ and belongs to $L^1([0,\\infty))$. Then $H=H_0+M_V$ is self-adjoint on $H^2$, both ordinary wave operators $\\Omega_\\pm=\\operatorname{s-lim}_{t\\to\\pm\\infty}e^{itH}e^{-itH_0}$ exist, $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{sc}}(H)=\\{0\\}$, and\n\n$$\\operatorname{ran}\\Omega_+=\\operatorname{ran}\\Omega_-=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm c}(H).$$\n\nHere $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}$ consists of vectors whose scalar PVM measures vanish on every Lebesgue null set; $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{sc}}$ consists of vectors with atomless singular scalar measures. The space $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{pp}}$ is the closed span of eigenspaces and $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm c}=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{pp}}^\\perp$. The scattering operator $S=\\Omega_+^*\\Omega_-$ is unitary on $L^2$.\n\nIn particular, all these conclusions hold for $V=V_{\\mathrm{loc}}+V_{\\mathrm{tail}}$, where $V_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ is compactly supported and $|V_{\\mathrm{tail}}(x)|\\le C(1+|x|)^{-1-\\delta}$ almost everywhere, with $\\delta>0$. For this class, for suitable finite constants and all sufficiently large $r$,\n\n$$h(r)\\le C_1r^{-1-\\delta}+C_2(1+r)^{3/2}e^{-c'r}.$$\n\nCoulomb decay $\\delta=0$ does not satisfy this sufficient decay hypothesis; no zero-energy nonresonance or absence-of-zero-eigenvalue hypothesis is imposed.",
